Say whether an observer was actually ENTERED, and stop the trace crashing the application.

CIDER_TRACE_KVO already existed in this file and I did not find it: my grep was rewritten by the
rtk hook and its compressed output dropped the matches, so I recorded in #194 that there was no KVO
tracing anywhere in vendor/src. That was wrong. What was there printed didChange, an observes line
on registration, and an array-observe with a backtrace.

WHAT IT COULD NOT ANSWER, and what #194 needed: whether a notification reaches an observer. There
was no trace on the dispatch, so "a property was set and nothing happened" could not be separated
into "nobody observes it" and "somebody does and the notification never arrived".

Added:
  CIDER_KVO notify     an observer is about to be entered, with the observer, the key path and the
                       observed object. This is the one that was missing.
  CIDER_KVO subclass   an NSKVONotifying_ subclass is created, which is the moment observation
                       actually takes effect on a class.
  CIDER_KVO will       now carries observed=, so an announcement with no observation info is
                       distinguishable from one with.
  CIDER_KVO addProperty the internal registration, which is what creates the subclass.

A PROBE MUST NOT BE ABLE TO CRASH WHAT IT MEASURES, and this one could. A KVO key is NOT always an
NSString: -willChangeValueForKey: is reached with a Class object among other things, and the
existing traces called -UTF8String on it unguarded. With CIDER_TRACE_KVO on, iA Writer died 52 lines
into launch with

  +[NSTextView UTF8String]: unrecognized selector sent to instance

four runs in a row, and every conclusion I drew from those runs was void: I reported that
addObserver was never called, that no subclass was ever created and that no observer was ever
notified. With the guard in place the same scenario runs to completion and reports 787 registrations,
44 subclasses and 569 notifications. The hazard was in code that predates this change; turning the
switch on is what made it reachable.

_CiderKVOText answers the class name when the object is not a string and "(nil)" when it is nil, so
every existing call site is hardened too.

Gate: darling-testsuite wired 67, built 67, ran 67, passed 66, failed 0, which is the baseline.

